part.text:
I have created a Next.js Server Action in `app/actions.ts` that safely handles creating a new post and invalidating the cache.

To guarantee that users do not see stale cached data on their next page load—an essential pattern often referred to as **read-your-own-writes**—the new Next.js function `updateTag` must be used instead of `revalidateTag`. 

Unlike `revalidateTag(..., "max")` which triggers a stale-while-revalidate background refresh and will serve stale cached data immediately after the mutation, `updateTag` immediately expires the cache. This ensures the next request waits for fresh data rather than serving old content.

Here is the server action:

```typescript
// app/actions.ts
'use server'

import { updateTag } from 'next/cache'

export async function createPost(formData: FormData) {
  // Logic to save the post to your database goes here...

  // Immediately expires the cache for 'posts' so the next 
  // page load blocks to fetch fresh data.
  updateTag('posts')
}
```
